  "status": "published",
  "title": "Rocket Stove",
  "summary": "An L-shaped burn tube that boils water on a handful of sticks. Built for camp and power cuts.",
  "difficulty": "intermediate",
  "estimatedHours": 5,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Use bare or painted mild steel only. Never use galvanized tube, because the zinc coating gives off toxic fumes when it is welded or heated.",
    "From the square tube, cut an 18 in riser and a 12 in feed tube, both measured on the long side, each with one end mitered at 45 degrees. Placed together, the miters form a closed 90 degree L.",
    "Grind the miter faces clean. Tack the L together, check it with the combination square, then weld all four seams fully so the joint is airtight. The draft depends on it.",
    "Build the fuel grate from the flat bar. Lay two 3 1/2 in crosspieces flat, and weld three 11 in bars standing on edge across them. Slide the grate into the feed tube so that 4 in sticks out as a shelf. The sticks rest about 1 1/8 in off the floor, and air feeds in underneath them.",
    "Weld four 4 in flat bar pot rests to the outside of the riser top, one per side, each standing 1 in above the rim so the flame has room to exit under the pot.",
    "Cut two 8 in skids from the angle and weld them across the bottom of the feed tube and the riser foot so the stove sits stable on uneven ground.",
    "Grind off the spatter and wire-brush the slag. Paint the outside with high-heat paint, then cure it with a small fire as the can directs.",
    "Before the first meal, do a full burn outdoors to cook off any remaining oil or paint. Only ever use it outdoors, because it produces carbon monoxide. Keep a bucket of water nearby."
  ],
